## ABSTRACT
The development of highly efficient separation technology for the purification of natural gas by removing ethane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\) and propane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\right)\) is a crucial but challenging task to their efficient utilization in the chemical industry and social life. Here, we report three isomorphic ultra-microporous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), M-pyz \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}\), Co, and Ni, and pyr = pyrazine) referred to as Fe-pyz, Co-pyz, and Ni-pyz, respectively, which possess high density of open metal sites and suitable pore structure. Compared with the benchmark materials reported, M-pyz not only has high adsorption capacities of \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) at low pressure (up to 51.6 and \(63.7\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-3}\)), but also exhibits excellent \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST) selectivities, TT and 2S, respectively. Theoretical calculations demonstrated that the materials' separation performance was driven by multiple intermolecular interactions (hydrogen bonding interactions and van der Waals effect) between gas molecules \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\right)\) and the M-pyz binding sites. And, dynamic breakthrough experiments verified the superior reusability and practical separation feasibility for the ternary \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) mixtures. Furthermore, M-pyz can be synthesized rapidly and on a large scale at room temperature. This work presents a series of promising MOFs adsorbents to efficiently purify natural gas and promotes the industrial development process of MOFs materials.
## KEYWORDS
natural gas, methane, adsorption separation, ultra-microporous, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)
## 1 Introduction
Natural gas, as a promising clean energy alternative or complementary to conventional fossil fuels, has been widely used in the industry and social life, owing to its high energy density of up to \(55.5\ \mathrm{MJ{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\), natural abundance, and environmental friendliness [1–3]. However, the presence of impurities such as a small amount of ethane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\) and propane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\right)\) in natural gas seriously affects the stability of pipeline transportation and storage [4–8]. Therefore, purification of natural gas by removing \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) is prerequisite for further efficient utilization. At present, the industry, as yet, has been using conventional cryogenic distillation to solve the relevant problems, but its huge energy consumption and safety problems are daunting. So it arouses growing attention to develop a highly efficient, safe, and energy-saving separation technology. The advanced adsorption separation technology based on the principle of physical adsorption shows a promising prospect owing to its advantages of simple operation, high efficiency, and low energy of regeneration, in which the development of efficient, stable, and readily available absorbents is the key to industrialization [9, 10]. Compared with traditional porous adsorbents such as zeolites, active carbon, porous silica gel, and resin, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed via the self-assembly of organic ligands and metal nodes, have garnered more significant attention due to their diverse structures and highly tunable pore systems and are considered as promising adsorbents for gas separation and purification [1,11–24]. In addition, the pore surface of MOFs can be designed with different recognition sites (e.g., open metal sites and specific organic functional groups) to regulate the host–guest interaction, thus improving the separation performance [25–28].
