Lavoisier-142A (MIL-142A) exhibits a high \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{8}/C H_{4}}\) (v:v, 50:50) selectivity (1300), but its \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}\) (v:v, 50:50) selectivity (13.7) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) absorption of \(0.51\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \((10.08\ \mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}c m^{-3}})\) at 0.1 bar make the removing of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) from natural gas difficult [37]. Similarly, boron cage based supramolecular metal-organic framework-2 (BSF-2) demonstrates high \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}\) (v:v, 50:50) selectivity (2609), while the adsorption capacities of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) are relatively low, only \(0.51\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \((14.28\ \mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}c m^{-3}})\) at 0.1 bar and \(1\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \((28.01\ \mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}c m^{-3}})\) at 0.05 bar, respectively [38]. In addition to the lack of separation performance, the large-scale synthesis of these materials also suffers from huge energy consumption and high cost, which hamper the industrial progress of MOFs adsorbents [39–42]. Consequently, there is an urgent need to develop high-performance MOFs that can be synthesized quickly and easily on a large scale at room temperature to realize the industrial application for natural gas purification.
Considering all factors comprehensively, we have synthesized three isomorphic MOFs, denoted as M-pyz (where \(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}\), Ni, and Fe, and \(\mathrm{pyz}=\) pyrazine). These MOFs with high-density open metal sites possess one-dimensional (1D) ultra-microporous channels and can be easily scaled up to the kilogram scale using a simple and eco-friendly room-temperature stirring synthesis method [43–46]. These MOFs exhibit high adsorption capacities of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\), especially at low pressures of 0.05 and 0.1 bar. The ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST) indicated that M-pyz showed the high separation ratio and separation potential \((\Delta q)\) for the \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}\) mixtures, which were comparable to many benchmark materials reported. The adsorption mechanism has been clearly verified by density functional theory (DFT) calculations and Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations. Notably, fixed bed breakthrough experiments further demonstrated that these MOFs are excellent sorbents for the natural gas purification with high stability. After at least 10 cycles, their performance does not decay, which surpasses most previously reported benchmark materials. This work not only provides a promising adsorbent material for natural gas purification, but also promotes the further industrial application of MOFs materials.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Structural analysis and characterization
The ultra-microporous material was prepared from pyrazine and potassium cyanide compounds through optimization and improvement methods based on previous literature reports [43, 47, 48]. The \(\mathrm{K_{2}[N i(C N)_{4}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}\) aqueous solution was added to the water/methanol solution of pyrazine and \(\mathrm{M(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\), stirred constantly for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\) at room temperature, and filtered dry to obtain the corresponding powder crystal product. The remarkably uncomplicated process has significant implications for future industrialization. The crystal structure and high purity were determined by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data (Fig. S1 in the ESM). Moreover, their high chemical stability has been demonstrated several times in previous reports, with crystal structures remaining intact long after exposure to air or immersion in water and organic solvents, and will not be repeated here [43, 48, 49]. M-pyz is thermally stable up to approximately \(350\ ^{\circ}{C},\) as evidenced by thermogravimetric analysis and variable temperature PXRD patterns (Figs. S1 and S2 in the ESM). The permanent porosity of activated M-pyz was confirmed by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\), exhibiting reversible type I sorption behavior without hysteresis and confirmed the microporous structures (Fig. 1(b)). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of Fe-pyz, Co-pyz, and Ni-pyz are 361, 458, and
\(572\ \mathrm{~m^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. Nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) aperture analysis shows that the pore sizes are 4, 3.8, and \(4\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\), respectively (Fig. 1(b)). Structural analysis shows that M-pyz possesses the ultra-micropore size around \(4.0\ \mathrm{~\AA~}\) (after subtracting van der Waals radii), consistent with the experimental value (Figs. 1(a) and 1(b), and Scheme S1 in the ESM), containing abundant open metal sites and cyano-groups neatly arranged on the channel surface, which can serve as strong adsorption sites (Table S2 in the ESM). Concretely, M(II) ion and four nitrogen atoms from four different \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}]^{2-}\) units form a four-connected planar geometric planar layer through coordination, supported by pyrazine organic ligands, and finally constitute a series of isomorphic three-dimensional (3D) framework structures with 1D ultra-micro channels. The combined effects of ultra-micropore shape and coordinatively unsaturated metal sites synergically endow M-pyz with strong binding interaction to \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\). The green and rapid synthesis method and excellent stability indicate that M-pyz is a promising porous adsorbent material for natural gas purification industrial applications.
## 2.2 Single-component adsorption experiments and selectivity calculations
