pyz) for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}\) (v:v, 50:50) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}\) (v:v, 50:50) mixtures, which are comparable to benchmark porous materials, such as ZUL-C2 (2.40 and \(2.60\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) [54], ZUL-C1 (2.53 and \(2.49\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) [54], and Shaanxi Normal University Bai's group78 (SNNU-Bai78) (2.54 and \(2.41\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) [35], outperform most reported benchmark adsorbents (Fig. 2(f) and Table S6 in the ESM). As a result, the robust M-pyz was a promising material for efficient separation of \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) over \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\).
## 2.3 Theoretical calculation
To enhance our understanding of the correlation between pore environment and alkane adsorption behaviors, we employed GCMC and DFT methodologies to identify adsorption sites and compute the associated binding energies. Consistent with the anticipated outcomes of the adsorption experiment, there was a significant difference in binding energies between different alkanes, with a trend of \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}>\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) in M-pyz. Among the three isomorphic MOFs, this work chooses Co-pyz as a representative to discuss relevant issues in depth. The optimal adsorption site for alkanes is located in the center of the channel (Fig. 3). Given the smaller molecular size, the \(\mathrm{C-H\cdotsNi}\) distance between \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) and the frameworks does not sufficiently approach to enable strong interactions (3.687–3.900 Å), resulting in correspondingly lower binding energies of \(31.94\;\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}}\). For \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\), there are multiple hydrogen bonding interactions between the gas molecule and organic linkers (C–H···N≡C 3.107–3.459 Å for \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and C–H···N≡C 2.815–3.153 Å for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\)) as well as the van der Waals effect between the gas and open metal sites (C–H···Ni 2.682–3.423 Å for \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and C–H···Ni 3.255–3.808 Å for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\)), which effectively trap \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) within the pore and result in significantly elevated binding energies of 44.79 and \(74.88\;\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}}\), respectively (Fig. 3). The calculated binding energies, while slightly higher than the experimental \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values, remain reasonable given the inherent limitations of DFT methods in terms of accuracy [61]. Based on the calculated binding energies, they are reasonably consistent with the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values, and the different binding energies attribute to the high selectivity of \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}\). In addition, the adsorption density distribution results under ambient conditions also indicate that M-pyz has a stronger affinity for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) than \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The theoretical calculations conducted in this study are in full accordance with the experimental findings discussed earlier, providing robust validation and reinforcing their coherence.
## 2.4 Dynamic breakthrough experiments
To validate the practical \(\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{8}}\) (v:v:v, 85:10:5) mixtures separation performance of M-pyz, breakthrough experiments were conducted on Fe-pyz, Co-pyz, and Ni-pyz at \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) with the flow rate set to be \(3\;\mathrm{mL{\cdot}min^{-1}}\). This hydrocarbon composition closely approximates the composition of natural gas under practical conditions. During the course of the experiments, \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) always penetrated immediately compared with \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) in three materials with the breakthrough time of \(22\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Fe-pyz, \(16\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Co-pyz, and \(11\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Ni-pyz. The results can be explained by the favorable affinity of M-pyz for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) in accordance with the single component adsorption isotherms in Figs. 2(a)–2(c). Notably, \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) was retained for \(97\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Fe-pyz, \(103\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Co-pyz, and \(89\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Ni-pyz, whereas \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) was retained for \(216\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Fe-pyz, \(197\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Co-pyz, and \(144\;\mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) on Ni-pyz (Figs. 4(a)–4(c)). According to the sample weight and gas velocity, the \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) productivity can be directly calculated. Consequently, it was worth noting that the \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) productivities (\(>99.99\%+\) purity) were \(8.54\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) in Fe-pyz, \(9.91\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) in Co-pyz, and \(8.88\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) in Ni-pyz, which are comparable to most of reported porous materials, such as ZUL-C2 (\(11.4\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [54], \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{TMBDC})(\mathrm{DABCO})_{0.5}\) (\(13.28\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [55], and SNNU-Bai78 (\(10.65\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [35], and outperform most reported adsorbents, e.g., ZUL-C1 (\(5.42\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [54], BSF-2 (\(3.09\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [38], BSF-1 (\(3.67\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [62], and MIL-142A (\(3.79\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)) [37] (Fig. S9e). Given the importance of M-pyz stability for industrial applications, the breakthrough experiments were repeated 10 times and the \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) production is almost constant (Fig. 4(d)). The results demonstrate that M-pyz exhibits excellent stability and cycle performance, as evidenced by the near-coincidence of the breakthrough curves. Thus, the material's separation performance remains consistently exceptional after cycle tests. To be suitable for industrial applications, \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) purification adsorbents must satisfy several requirements such as strong affinity for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) (\(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\)), high selectivity, large \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) productivity, and excellent chemical and thermal stability and scalability for easy production. The study comprehensively compared these criteria between Co-pyz and other previously reported materials (Figs. 2(e) and 2(f), and Fig. S7 and Table S7 in the ESM). The results showed that unlike other MOFs that excel in one or more of the above criteria, M-pyz meets all of them, making it an ideal candidate for industrial \(\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}\) separation.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, three excellent robust ultra-microporous MOFs adsorbents M-pyz were synthesized by an easy scalability method and exhibit a high \(C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) absorption capacity and separation performance of \(\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}\) mixtures. Theoretical calculations indicate that the main binding sites of gas molecules are located between two adjacent open metal sites. The selective adsorption behavior of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) and \(C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) is due to the hydrogen bonding interactions \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}\cdots\mathrm{N}\equiv\mathrm{C})\) and van der Waals effect between the gas molecules and the frameworks. Breakthrough experimental results support M-pyz as a promising candidate for \(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}\) separation. Additionally, M-pyz boasts several advantages, such as a simple and cost-effective synthesis process, excellent chemical and thermal stability, and strong reusability, which render it a very promising choice for natural gas purification and greatly promote the industrial application process of MOFs materials.
